WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.559
Idea. When you buy a drink with cash, two things happen: you get a drink, and your cash goes down. Every business transaction is like this. It is recorded twice, once as a debit and once as a credit, for the same amount.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:29.403
Debit the account that receives value: an asset that goes up, an expense, or drawings. Credit the account that gives value: an asset that goes down, income, a liability that goes up, or equity that goes up.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:41.326
Say a shop buys goods to sell for three hundred dollars, paying from the bank. Purchases receives the goods, so debit purchases. The bank gives the money, so credit bank.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:52.064
Method. First, name the two accounts the transaction touches. Then ask which one receives value: debit it. And which one gives value: credit it.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:01:05.531
Next, check where the money came from: business cash, the business bank, or the owner's own pocket. If the owner pays a business cost with private money, credit equity, because the owner has put more in.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:14.493
Finally, pick the option with your debit and your credit in the right columns. Many wrong options use the same two accounts, swapped.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:27.033
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2022. A trader takes goods from the business for personal use. Which account is debited, and which is credited?

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.849
The owner is taking value out of the business for personal use. That is drawings, so debit drawings.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:46.730
The goods were bought earlier and recorded in purchases. They will not be sold now, so reduce purchases by crediting it. Do not credit sales: nothing was sold, and no income was earned.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:56.268
So the answer is A: debit drawings, credit purchases. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:03.831
Common mistake. A common mistake is crediting sales for goods the owner takes. No sale happened.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.375
Another is crediting bank when the owner paid with private money. The owner has put more in, so credit equity.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:23.518
Your turn. Now you try. Nadia runs a flower shop. For each of the three transactions, decide the account to debit and the account to credit. Pause and decide.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:40.213
Answer. The van is an asset the business receives: debit motor vehicles, and the bank gives, so credit bank. Cash taken by the owner: debit drawings, credit cash. Electricity is an expense: debit electricity, credit cash.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:50.849
Remember: name the two accounts, debit the one that receives, credit the one that gives, and check where the money came from. Now try the practice questions.
